457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 295 0 R
/T (At Roots Foundation, we have always believed that true change begins at the grassroots. This year has reaffirmed that belief, as communities themselves stepped forward, not just as participants, but as co-creators of transformation. What makes our approach different is how we build every solution for communities, with their equal participation. We start by listening and understanding their realities, aspirations, and challenges and then co-design interventions that truly belong to them. Only after this process do we seek partnerships and funding to bring these solutions to life. Our focus has always been on creating programs that endure. We work with our partners to ensure that each initiative runs for at least three to four years, long enough to build local capacity, enable behaviour change, and ensure that the project becomes self-reliant and its impact continues well beyond our exit. In Sustainable Agriculture, our work now spans more than 96,000 acres, but what matters most is the mindset shift, farmers choosing to adopt Direct Seeding of Rice and crop residue management because they have witnessed their neighbours save costs, conserve water, and inspire entire clusters to give up burning. In Sports, over 10,000 children have been engaged across our programs. The real success lies not in medals alone, but in the fact that leagues like the Veer Champions League are helping children stay in school, while barefoot athletes in Jharkhand are competing at national arenas, carrying with them the pride of their villages. In Education and Skilling, the progress has been equally profound. Through programs like LEAP and Unnati, young people are building confidence, finding direction, and redefining what empowerment means, not just in livelihoods, but in dignity, voice, and independence. Our Community Development programs continue to strengthen local systems through healthcare, scholarships, digital literacy, and livelihoods, each effort reinforcing that empowerment is not delivered; it is nurtured until communities take ownership and lead. This year, we also strengthened our monitoring and evaluation systems by onboarding independent experts from the industry. Regardless of donor mandates, we are getting our projects third-party assessed against best-practice frameworks because transparency and accountability are integral to how we measure change. Across all our states of work, we are also deepening collaboration with state governments to align our programs with public systems, ensuring that behaviour change and continuity go hand in hand. As Roots Foundation continues to grow as a trusted development partner, our greatest achievement remains the same: when a community no longer needs us, because it has found the strength, skills, and systems to sustain its own progress.)
/E (At Roots Foundation, we have always believed that true change begins at the grassroots. This year has reaffirmed that belief, as communities themselves stepped forward, not just as participants, but as co-creators of transformation. What makes our approach different is how we build every solution for communities, with their equal participation. We start by listening and understanding their realities, aspirations, and challenges and then co-design interventions that truly belong to them. Only after this process do we seek partnerships and funding to bring these solutions to life. Our focus has always been on creating programs that endure. We work with our partners to ensure that each initiative runs for at least three to four years, long enough to build local capacity, enable behaviour change, and ensure that the project becomes self-reliant and its impact continues well beyond our exit. In Sustainable Agriculture, our work now spans more than 96,000 acres, but what matters most is the mindset shift, farmers choosing to adopt Direct Seeding of Rice and crop residue management because they have witnessed their neighbours save costs, conserve water, and inspire entire clusters to give up burning. In Sports, over 10,000 children have been engaged across our programs. The real success lies not in medals alone, but in the fact that leagues like the Veer Champions League are helping children stay in school, while barefoot athletes in Jharkhand are competing at national arenas, carrying with them the pride of their villages. In Education and Skilling, the progress has been equally profound. Through programs like LEAP and Unnati, young people are building confidence, finding direction, and redefining what empowerment means, not just in livelihoods, but in dignity, voice, and independence. Our Community Development programs continue to strengthen local systems through healthcare, scholarships, digital literacy, and livelihoods, each effort reinforcing that empowerment is not delivered; it is nurtured until communities take ownership and lead. This year, we also strengthened our monitoring and evaluation systems by onboarding independent experts from the industry. Regardless of donor mandates, we are getting our projects third-party assessed against best-practice frameworks because transparency and accountability are integral to how we measure change. Across all our states of work, we are also deepening collaboration with state governments to align our programs with public systems, ensuring that behaviour change and continuity go hand in hand. As Roots Foundation continues to grow as a trusted development partner, our greatest achievement remains the same: when a community no longer needs us, because it has found the strength, skills, and systems to sustain its own progress.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
